. *****************************************************************************
> ***
. ****************** Do-File H2: #ManyDaughters Project - RT 0139pp ***********
> ***
. *****************************************************************************
> ***
. 
. * Authors: [REDACTED], [REDACTED]
. 
. /* Summary
> 
> We followed the pre-analysis plan (PAP) as initially registered. 
> 
> The only deviation concerns the sample selection: we restricted the sample to
>  respondents living in private households (keep if inlist(pop, 1, 2)), based 
> on the pop variable in ppathl. At the time of writing the PAP, this classific
> ation was not identified due to lack of data access. 
> 
> In addition, following Footnote 1 of the PAP, we included a dummy for East Ge
> rmany using the sampreg variable from ppathl. Although we were initially unsu
> re whether sampreg would be available for all individuals—since this was not 
> clearly documented—we confirmed its availability and proceeded accordingly.  
> 
> 
> */
. 
. * ============================
. * Setup * 
. * ============================
. 
. * clear all
. version 18 // indicate the version of stata you are using here

. set maxvar 10000 // extend the maximum number of variables to 10,000


. 
. * INSTALL PACKAGES * 
. * install all packages you need here, for example:
. *ssc install outreg2
. 
. 
. 
. 
. 
. * CONFIGURE FILE PATHS * 
. /*
> * the following code automatically sets paths based on where you saved the di
> stributed file-package on your system *
> if strpos("`c(pwd)'", "code") {
>     global ROOT "`c(pwd)'/.."
> }
> else {
>     global ROOT "`c(pwd)'"
> }
> global IN "$ROOT/data/raw" // folder, in which the original datasets are stor
> ed
> global DATA "$ROOT/data/derived" // folder for datasets
> global OUT "$ROOT/out" // folder for output (results, tables)
> 
> */
. 
. * ============================
. * Data Preparation * 
. * ============================
. 
. * LOAD AND MERGE DATA * 
. 
.         use "$IN/ppathl.dta", clear // use ppathl as a starting dataset
(SOEP-Core, v37 (EU Edition), doi:10.5684/soep.core.v37eu)

. 
.         * Merge ppathl with all datasets, that you deem relevant for your ana
> lysis. (Tip: use keepusing() to specify which variables to keep from the usin
> g dataset; the default is all.)
. 
.         * biobirth contains information on fertility history
.         merge m:1 pid using "$IN/biobirth.dta", nogen keep(master matches) ke
> epusing(kidsex01-kidsex19 kidgeb01-kidgeb19 kidmon01-kidmon19)
(variable pid was long, now double to accommodate using data's values)
(label sex already defined)
(label sex_EN already defined)

    Result                      Number of obs
    -----------------------------------------
    Not matched                             0
    Matched                           663,123  
    -----------------------------------------

.         * pgen contains user-friendly data on the individual level that are c
> onsolidated from different sources
.         merge 1:1 pid syear using "$IN/pgen.dta", nogen keep(master matches) 
> keepusing(pgcasmin pgfamstd pgemplst pgmonth)
(variable syear was long, now double to accommodate using data's values)

    Result                      Number of obs
    -----------------------------------------
    Not matched                             0
    Matched                           663,123  
    -----------------------------------------

.         * pl contains all variables from the individual questionnaire
.         merge 1:1 pid syear using "$IN/pl.dta", nogen keep(master matches) ke
> epusing( plh0004)
(label cid_EN already defined)
(label pid_EN already defined)
(label cid already defined)

    Result                      Number of obs
    -----------------------------------------
    Not matched                             0
    Matched                           663,123  
    -----------------------------------------

.         * hgen contains generated variables on household level from the house
> hold interview; use for household net income
.         merge m:1 hid syear using "$IN/hgen.dta", nogen keep(master matches) 
> keepusing(hid syear hghinc)
(label iyear already defined)
(label iyear_EN already defined)

    Result                      Number of obs
    -----------------------------------------
    Not matched                             0
    Matched                           663,123  
    -----------------------------------------

. 
.         label language EN // change language of the labels to English

. 
.         save "$DATA/h2_dataset.dta", replace // save merged dataset
file C:\Users\dalbrecht\Research
    Projects\ManyDaughters\ManyDaughters_DataPreprocessing/data/derived_confi
    > dential/95%-sample/h2_dataset.dta saved

. 
. 
.         
.         
. * ============================
. * Analysis
. * ============================
. use "$DATA/h2_dataset.dta", clear

. 
. * FILTERS * (
.  
.         *only keep participants from private households
.         keep if inlist(pop,1,2)
(89,279 observations deleted)

.         
.         ****** For Hypothesis 4, we restrict the sample to respondents observ
> ed in the 2005, 2009,2014,2019 wave, which includes a survey question about p
> olitical preferences
.         * Keep only waves where the outcome is available
.         keep if inlist(syear, 2005, 2009, 2014, 2019)
(469,331 observations deleted)

. 
.         * Keep only valid responses to political self-placement (0–10)
.         keep if inrange(plh0004, 0, 10)
(18,622 observations deleted)

. 
.         ***** We include respondent who are biological parents at the time of
>  the interview (biolparenti,2018 = 1, as described in Table 1) Biological par
> ent. Dummy variable equal to 1 if the respondent has at least one child at ti
> me t: kidgeb01 ≤ t.
.         gen biolparent = 0

.         replace biolparent = 1 if kidgeb01 < syear & kidgeb01 > 0
(59,895 real changes made)

.         replace biolparent = 1 if kidgeb01 == syear & kidmon01 <= pgmonth & k
> idmon01 > 0
(216 real changes made)

.         keep if biolparent == 1
(25,780 observations deleted)

. 
. 
. * GENERATE VARIABLES *
. 
. * Generate reverse-coded outcome: higher = more left-leaning
. gen left_score = 11 - plh0004

. 
. 
.         * generate dummy variable for whether participant has at least one da
> ughter (1) or not (0)
.         * hasdaughterit Dummy variable equal to 1 if the respondent’s first b
> iological child is a daughter at time t, based on kidsex01 = 2 and kidgeb01 ≤
>  t
.         * we keep the variable name as d_daughter
.         gen d_daughter = (kidsex01 == 2 & kidgeb01 <= syear & kidgeb01 > 0)

. 
.         
.         
.         *set of three dummy variables indicating whether the respondenthas tw
> o, three, or four or more children.
.         gen numchildren = 0

.                 forvalues i = 1/19 {
  2.                         local var = string(`i', "%02.0f")
  3.                         quietly replace numchildren = numchildren + ///
>                         (!missing(kidgeb`var') & kidgeb`var' > 0 & kidgeb`var
> ' <= syear)
  4. }

. 
.         gen child2      = (numchildren == 2)

.         gen child3      = (numchildren == 3)

.         gen child4plus  = (numchildren >= 4)

. 
.         
.         *CONTROLS
. 
.         * three dummies for the age of the first children
.         
.         gen firstchild_age = syear - kidgeb01 if kidgeb01 > 0 & kidgeb01 <= s
> year

.         gen first0_4    = inrange(firstchild_age, 0, 4)

.         gen first5_9     = inrange(firstchild_age, 5, 9)

.         gen first10_14   = inrange(firstchild_age, 10, 14)

. 
.         
.         * Generate respondent age
.         gen age = syear - gebjahr if gebjahr > 0 & gebjahr <= syear

. 
.         * Generate squared age
.         gen age_sqrt = age^2 if !missing(age)

. 
.         * Generate male dummy (1 = male, 0 = female), exclude invalid/missing
>  values
.         gen male = .
(60,111 missing values generated)

.         replace male = 1 if sex == 1
(26,805 real changes made)

.         replace male = 0 if sex == 2
(33,304 real changes made)

. 
.         * Create two education dummies based on pgcasmin
.         * Drop respondents with no answer to education
.         *** SHOULD WE DROP??????
.         drop if pgcasmin < 0 | missing(pgcasmin)
(538 observations deleted)

. 
.         * Generate education dummies
.         gen edu_high     = inrange(pgcasmin, 6, 7)

. 
.         gen edu_tertiary = inrange(pgcasmin, 8, 9)

.         * Reference category: CASMIN 0–5 (lower secondary or less)
. 
.         
.         ***five marital status dummies
.         * Drop invalid or missing values only
.         drop if pgfamstd < 0 | missing(pgfamstd)
(186 observations deleted)

. 
.         * Generate marital status dummies
.         gen married         = inlist(pgfamstd, 1, 7)

.         gen married_sep     = inlist(pgfamstd, 2, 8)

.         gen divorced        = (pgfamstd == 4)

.         gen widowed         = (pgfamstd == 5)

.         gen partner_abroad  = (pgfamstd == 6)

.         * Reference category: Single (pgfamstd == 3)
. 
.         
.         
.         *employment status
.         
.         * Drop invalid or missing responses
.         drop if pgemplst < 0 | missing(pgemplst)
(0 observations deleted)

. 
.         * Generate employment status dummy
.         gen employed = inlist(pgemplst, 1, 2)

. 
. 
.         * the logarithm of household income
.         * Drop invalid or missing household income
.         drop if hghinc < 0 | missing(hghinc)
(2,741 observations deleted)

. 
.         * Generate log of household net income (add 1 to avoid log(0))
.         gen ln_hhinc = ln(hghinc + 1)

. 
.         
.         *dummy for being born in Germany.
.         * Drop invalid or missing values
.         drop if germborn < 0 | missing(germborn)
(0 observations deleted)

. 
.         * Generate dummy: 1 if born in Germany or immigrated before 1950
.         gen germanborn = (germborn == 1)

. 
.         
.         * Genenrate dummy east/west (see footnote 1 in the PAP)
.         * Drop missing or invalid sampreg values
.         drop if missing(sampreg) | sampreg < 0
(19 observations deleted)

. 
.         * Generate East Germany dummy
.         gen east = (sampreg == 2)

. 
. 
. *****************************************************************************
> ********
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H2 * 
. 
. /*
> We estimate a pooled linear Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) regression model to 
> examine whether having a daughter as the first child influences political ori
> entation. The dependent variable is based on the SOEP item plh0004, which ask
> s respondents to place themselves on a political left–right scale ranging fro
> m 0 (left) to 10 (right). We reverse-code the responses as 11 − plh0004 so th
> at higher values indicate more left-leaning preferences.
> 
> The analysis is restricted to respondents interviewed in 2005, 2009, 2014, or
>  2019 who were living in private households, had at least one biological chil
> d already born at the time of the interview, and provided a valid response to
>  the political orientation question. The key independent variable is d_daught
> er, a binary indicator equal to 1 if the respondent’s first biological child 
> is a daughter born on or before the interview date, and 0 if the first child 
> is a son.
> 
> The model includes a set of control variables accounting for family compositi
> on, demographics, and socioeconomic characteristics. These include indicators
>  for the number of children (two, three, or four or more, with one child as t
> he reference category), the age group of the first child (0–4, 5–9, 10–14), r
> espondent’s age and age squared, sex (male), education level (upper secondary
>  and tertiary, with lower education as the reference), marital status (marrie
> d, separated, divorced, widowed, partner abroad; single as the reference), em
> ployment status (employed), the logarithm of net monthly household income, a 
> dummy for being born in Germany or having immigrated before 1950, and a regio
> nal indicator for East Germany.
> 
> We estimate the model including wave fixed effects to account for period-spec
> ific shocks, and standard errors are clustered at the individual level. A pos
> itive and statistically significant coefficient on d_daughter (β₁ > 0, p < 0.
> 05) will be interpreted as evidence supporting the hypothesis that having a d
> aughter as the first child increases left-leaning political preferences.
> 
> */
.         
. * Estimate OLS with robust standard errors
. reg left_score ///
>     d_daughter ///
>     child2 child3 child4plus ///
>     first0_4 first5_9 first10_14 ///
>     age age_sqrt male ///
>     edu_high edu_tertiary ///
>     married married_sep divorced widowed partner_abroad ///
>     employed ln_hhinc germanborn east ///
>     i.syear, vce(cluster pid)

Linear regression                               Number of obs     =     56,627
                                                F(24, 31224)      =      56.20
                                                Prob > F          =     0.0000
                                                R-squared         =     0.0387
                                                Root MSE          =     1.5943

